Prior art

The techniques this engine implements are decades-old published computer
graphics work, described in academic papers, textbooks and articles long
before this project existed:

  * Binary space partitioning, and its use for solid geometry and
    rendering order -- Fuchs, Kedem & Naylor (1980); Naylor, Amanatides
    & Thibault (1990).
  * Portal-based potentially-visible-set precomputation -- Seth Teller's
    1992 Berkeley dissertation, "Visibility Computations in Densely
    Occluded Polyhedral Environments".
  * Radiosity and lightmap baking -- Goral et al. (1984) and the large
    literature that followed.
  * Constructive solid geometry on convex brushes; Sutherland-Hodgman
    polygon clipping (1974); Gribb-Hartmann frustum plane extraction.

id Software's Quake tools and Valve's Source engine are well-known
implementations of several of these, and their published source has
informed the *structure* of some modules here -- most visibly the BSP
split heuristic, the portal-flow separating-plane test, the winding
clipper, and the player movement solver. Those modules were written from
scratch in Rust against the descriptions of the algorithms; no code was
copied. Where behaviour is deliberately matched (Quake's inverted pitch
convention, the texture base-axis table, Source's 30-unit air speed cap),
the source comments say so explicitly, because a reader deserves to know
which choices are engineering and which are compatibility.

